e John  Cage reference because when we started  working on the film you know we the  first thing we did is we saw the first  assembly basically of the movie and I  think what we both Eric and I connected  to most strongly was the love between  these characters that you that you feel  and we thought how can we lean into this  the intimacy that you feel between these  characters in this tangible love between  these these members of these of this  family that are so desperately trying to  survive and so the first thing we we  started doing was stripping out music  because there was basically wall to wall  tent music and once we did that we  realized how can we get closer to these  characters and with the daughter Regan's  character she's she's deaf in the movie  and she's played by an actress who's  deaf in real life Millicent Simmons and  John described to us how Millie onset  became kind of the spirit guide for that  for the actors  she taught all the actors American Sign  Language which they use in the movie and  so when we started working with the  sound design on the film we were  thinking how we want to get closer to  these characters we want to feel what  they're feeling and with Millie's  character we were like okay she's deaf  this is a world where sound is  everything if you make too much sound  you will get killed and so in a way  Millie became our spirit guide for sort  of unlocking the sound design to the  film because  we had a description from John of  Millie's experience of which which how  she experiences the world when her  cochlear implant is turned on and he and  she described it as being sort of a low  Rumble almost you know hearing as touch  in a way and Eric and I immediately  related it to our own experiences being  in anechoic chambers where as eric has  described what you really hear once your  ears  open up after five or ten minutes is the  sound of you know your own body your  heart faintly pumping in the background  your nervous system sort